<TargetEndpoint name="backends">
  <PreFlow name="PreFlow">
    <Request/>
    <Response/>
  </PreFlow>

  <!--
    LoadBalancer + Server name references the "backends" Target Server
    (terraform/7-apigee-runtime/main.tf, google_apigee_target_server.backends),
    whose host is the PSC endpoint attachment's own address — never a
    literal IP or a public *.run.app URL here. This is Google's own
    documented pattern for Apigee X -> Cloud Run over PSC:
    https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/serverless/connecting-to-cloud-run-from-apigee-x-using-psc

    The Audience element uses ref=, not inline {variable} templating —
    GoogleIDToken's Audience element explicitly rejects the latter
    ("The Audience element must not contain variables"). The referenced
    flow variable (audience) is set by whichever KVM-Get*Audience step
    matched in the ProxyEndpoint PreFlow, from the backend-audiences KVM
    (terraform creates the empty container; apigee/kvm/backend-audiences.json
    supplies the real per-backend Cloud Run URLs via ci/deploy-apigee-config.sh).
  -->
  <HTTPTargetConnection>
    <!--
      WITHOUT THIS BLOCK THIS TARGET USES APIGEE'S 55s DEFAULT, and that default
      governed every northbound call — Translation, Sales-Agent and NaaS alike —
      because it is the tightest limit in a chain whose other layers all allow
      more:

        this target                         55s   <— was the real ceiling
        backend Cloud Run timeout          300s
        LB apigee-northbound timeout_sec   360s   (terraform/5-network-psc)
        BFF upstream_timeout_seconds       420s   (aihub-ui bff/app/config.py)
        BFF Cloud Run timeout              600s

      Translation and Sales-Agent largely got away with it: their API call
      enqueues a Cloud Tasks job and returns immediately, so the slow work
      happens on a worker that is not on this path. NaaS has no worker — its
      /chat turn is synchronous SSE chaining MCP tool calls and LLM calls — so
      55s would have been hit routinely.

      330s sits above the backends' own 300s Cloud Run timeout and below the
      load balancer's 360s. Every layer outward must stay more generous than
      the one inside it; change these together or the wall just moves. The
      same reasoning, and the same mistake, is recorded for the southbound
      path in terraform/5-network-psc/main.tf:168-198 and BUILD-LOG #43 —
      that fix set vertex-gemini-target's timeout and the LB, and nobody ever
      set this northbound equivalent.
    -->
    <Properties>
      <Property name="io.timeout.millis">330000</Property>
    </Properties>
    <LoadBalancer>
      <Server name="backends"/>
    </LoadBalancer>
    <!--
      Apigee does NOT forward its own BasePath (/api, see proxies/default.xml)
      to the target: the target path is <Path> + proxy.pathsuffix, and
      pathsuffix excludes the BasePath. Without this element the backends
      receive /translation/v1/translate, while both apps mount their routes
      under API_PREFIX = /api/translation/v1 and /api/sales/v1 respectively
      (Translation/.env.api.prod, Sales-Agent/.env.api.prod) — so every call
      404'd at the Cloud Run service, having passed cleanly through the whole
      proxy. docs/20 §6.4 asserted the two matched "with no path rewrite";
      that was wrong, and it stayed invisible because no request had ever
      reached a backend before 2026-09-11.

      This MUST stay in step with backend-urlmap's path rules
      (terraform/6-workloads/6c-gclt-aicoe-dev-ingress/main.tf), which match
      on /api/translation/* and /api/sales/*. If this element is removed or
      the rules are reverted to /translation/*, neither rule matches and
      everything silently falls through to that url map's default_service
      (translation) — Sales-Agent traffic would be answered by the
      Translation service rather than failing. See docs/BUILD-LOG.md #38.
    -->
    <Path>/api</Path>
    <Authentication>
      <GoogleIDToken>
        <Audience ref="audience"/>
        <!--
          Google omits the `email` claim from a minted ID token unless it is
          explicitly requested. Both backends check that claim against their
          APIGEE_RUNTIME_SA_EMAIL setting (src/api/core/apigee_auth.py) to prove
          the caller really is Apigee and not some other holder of a valid
          run.invoker token — so without this the claim arrives empty and every
          request is rejected with "Apigee identity token email mismatch: got
          '', expected 'apigee-int-runtime@...'", surfacing to the user as
          "Unexpected caller identity". Confirmed live 2026-09-11; the audience
          check passes at that point, so it is specifically the identity half
          of the backend's authorization that fails. See docs/BUILD-LOG.md #38.
        -->
        <IncludeEmail>true</IncludeEmail>
      </GoogleIDToken>
    </Authentication>
  </HTTPTargetConnection>
</TargetEndpoint>
